module poisoned_slot.neg_1

// A NAME WHOSE INITIALIZER WAS REFUSED, THEN USED IN AN INTERPOLATION SLOT.
// One mistake is seeded here -- `mystery` is not a callable novac knows -- so
// exactly one diagnostic may come out, and it must be that one.
//
// WHAT THIS CELL GUARDS, and it is not the wording: the second half of the
// cascade rule. `check/binds.nv` refuses the initializer and STILL binds the
// name, with `no_ty()`, precisely so that later uses do not raise a second
// complaint about an unknown name. That promise binds every consumer, and the
// interpolation slot is the consumer that broke it.
//
// MEASURED 2026-09-20, and the measurement is why the file exists. The binding
// half landed on 2026-09-19 (398cdb1ab) and turned these three lines into an
// INTERNAL COMPILER ERROR -- `types.nv:244: kind_of asked for a type id outside
// the interner` -- because `no_ty()` is `TyId(-1)` and the display doors ask a
// poisoned type for its kind. The build of 2026-09-17 gave two honest
// diagnostics on the same source. So a fix that removed a cosmetic second
// complaint bought a crash: strictly worse, because a refusal tells the reader
// which form is not compiled yet while an ice tells them the compiler is broken
// and nothing about their program.
//
// AND THE REACH IS WHY IT IS NOT A SMALL BUG. An ice aborts the whole run, so a
// batch `novac check` stopped at the first file that hit it, and every count of
// "how far is self-hosting" became a function of argument order: 3, 15, 935 and
// 1495 diagnostics over one unchanged tree (registry No. 1168, No. 1169).
//
// THE CELL IS NEGATIVE ON PURPOSE. It is not waiting for `mystery` to become
// callable -- it is waiting for nothing. Should the subset one day compile the
// form, this file must be rewritten around another refused initializer rather
// than deleted: the property is "a poisoned type reaches a consumer and the
// compiler still answers with ONE named refusal", and that property has no
// expiry date.

fn f() -> str {
    ro from = mystery(1)
    "a ${from} b"
}
